90:+It leaves model weights fixed and edits the harness through eight mounted files: system prompt, guardrails, memory, tool guidance, subagents, skills, scripts and a plugin. **Task-level recursion** (Propose, Probe, Compose): sample a reference run of the current harness, draft two candidate harnesses (an "invariant" one about how the agent works and a "domain" one about what a practitioner knows), roll each out once on the same task, and have an editor model read the full trajectories and compose one harness. The paper says contrasts "inform subsequent composition rather than verify task correctness". **Harnessing-level recursion** stores the adopted harness plus an `EXPERIENCE.md` lesson in a per-domain library that later tasks read. Its benchmarks (JobBench, LAB, WorkBuddy-Bench) are professional-work tasks, not coding-agent tasks, and its executors are OpenCode and Codex. It needs Python 3.12, Vertex AI credentials and, for one benchmark, Docker.
122:+What Harness² does (read in `library.py` and `prompts.py`): an entry is the full adopted harness tree plus `EXPERIENCE.md` with `cue`, `lesson`, `evidence`, `confidence`; at most one lesson per task; the reflector may write `NO_ENTRY` and is told adoption "alone never establishes that an edit helped or will transfer"; each edit gets a verdict of `helped`, `hurt`, `no observed effect` or `confounded`, with `confounded` preferred when one rollout is all the support; a lesson resting only on confounded rows must be narrowed or declined; the cue must describe the "shape of situation", neither generic nor task-specific. Scope control: libraries are per domain and per run (each sweep starts empty), retrieval is by browsing an `INDEX.md` rather than injection, executable components are kept "rather than summarised as prose", and a tripwire refuses grader-verdict text in any context or lesson. The paper's own ablation: removing the library costs 3.1 points on WorkBuddy (77.5 to 74.4) and 1.8 on JobBench (36.0 to 34.2); a JobBench library helped WorkBuddy refinement by 0.5 to 2.9 points.
